title The guard caught a peer on its first rebase
prompt The send of `20260907.000903`, which took three rebases and taught its own lesson.
think The work log was written before the send, so it records the round and not the sending. Three rebases moved real facts, and dated testimony is not rewritten -- so this is the send's own record beside it, rather than an edit to the first.
think Rebase one: xy had moved six commits. Four files conflicted, and the ledger one was the interesting one -- a peer had folded the SAME four rows I folded (%504, %513, %518, %520) into three shelves, and theirs published first. Withdrew my fold whole and took theirs, which is REDS `%515`'s own recorded move applied to a fold rather than to a number.
think Rebase two and three: xy moved again each time. The pin needed a fold regardless, so I aimed the second one at three DIFFERENT rows -- %523, %527, %528, the oldest still foldable -- and left my own two fresh rows on the pin where peers can see them.
obs MY OWN GUARD CAUGHT A PEER'S INSTANCE ON ITS FIRST REBASE, and that is the whole proof. `shim_reason` went green on the tree I built it in, then the rebase brought `tools/r/rota_declared_witness.rish` -- a guard a peer had landed that same hour -- and the reading answered `reason_lost_rostered=1`, naming the file and its control. The population also moved 8 to 10, because peers wrote two more stderr-reporting controls while I worked. A guard that only ever reads zero on the tree that authored it has proven nothing about anywhere else; this one found a live instance in a lane I do not hold, within the hour, and the repair was the same single line.
obs AND I MADE THAT REPAIR RATHER THAN NAMING IT, on the test the peer log stated yesterday: a peer's file is mine to touch when the repair is COMPUTED rather than chosen. There is exactly one answer here -- interpolate `${ctl.err}` beside `${ctl.out}`, the identical line five other witnesses took an hour earlier -- and leaving it would have reddened a rostered gate on all eight ships. Where the earlier broken CHECKPOINTS link had three candidate shelves and its finder rightly left it alone, this has one.
obs RULE 4 PAID, AND NOW IT IS PRICED. My two rows were numbered THREE times: %524 and %525 from the anointed spine, then %529 and %530 when peers spent both, then %530 and %531 when a peer took %529 as well. Each renumber cost exactly one edit to `construction/REDS.md` and NOTHING else -- I grepped every living surface I had written, the card, the shelf, both roster blocks, the session log, the scan and control headers, and the commit body, and not one of them spelled a number. Every one cited the stamp `20260907.000030`, which never moved. That is the card's fifteen-times-asked question answered with a measurement rather than an opinion: the elder cost was 19 files on a first renumber and 10 on a third, and this was zero on all three, because the citations were stamps before the first collision arrived.
obs THE RENUMBER ITSELF WAS MATCHED ON THE STAMP, NEVER ON THE NUMBER ALONE. REDS `%519` records a blind `sed` that rewrote a peer's row, so each substitution named `**REDS %530 (\`20260907.000030\`)` in full and asserted a count of exactly one before replacing, and the higher number moved first so the two could not collide with each other mid-edit.
obs A DROPPED ROW WOULD HAVE BEEN INVISIBLE, AND A GUARD ANSWERED THAT. Resolving the third rebase I took MY side of the ledger conflict whole, which is the resolution most likely to silently drop a row a peer added in the same hunk. Rather than reading the hunk harder, I let `reds_ledger_monotone_witness` answer: it holds 1..N with no gaps across the pin and every fold archive, so a dropped row reds it. GREEN, so nothing went missing -- checked rather than believed.
obs FOUR REDS AT THE OPEN, ZERO AT THE CLOSE. The lap opened `run_verdict=guard_red` with `fleet_watch`, `reds_ledger_monotone`, `reds_ledger_headline` and `standing_equipment` red, and three of those four were one root -- a stale base missing `%523`. The full re-verify after the rebase read 158 guards, 155 green, 0 red, 3 gated at custody, 1,090s.
obs THE LEDGER HEADLINE IS REGENERATED BY THE PRE-COMMIT HOOK, WHICH A REBASE SKIPS -- so it drifted after every one of the three, and the amend only repaired it when `construction/REDS.md` was itself in the staged set. `sh tools/fixtures/r/reds_ledger_headline_write.sh write` is the direct call, and running it by hand after each rebase is what kept the monotone guard honest. That is REDS `%339`'s shape met three times in one send.
loom send=twice_pulled rebases=3 conflicts=10 renumbers=3 citation_sweeps=0
loom pass=full_reverify guards=158 green=155 red=0 gated=3 wall_s=1090
file tools/r/rota_declared_witness.rish a peer's guard, the same one-line repair, found by the guard this round seated
file session-logs/date/20260907/20260907-010542_the-guard-caught-a-peer-on-its-first-rebase.kyri this record
file construction/ITINERARY.md the Git nib carried forward, since a follow-up commit becomes the parent
recommend keep-going -- landed on both remotes at `0cbb8457b` with the full roster green; the next lap in this lane is the mark and digest the cellar's own header promises when sealed.
